# A hook is executed by its shebang, and `#!/bin/sh\r` is not a program any
# kernel finds. Git for Windows checks text out as CRLF by default, so without
# this line the hook installs cleanly on Windows and then fails on every commit
# with an error naming a file that plainly exists.
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# The nightly's pages are generated, and git already refuses to auto-merge them --
# but it refuses hunk by hunk. #446 is what that costs: a hand-merge of the 25th's
# page against main faced 22 separate conflicts in one file, and one of them was
# resolved by keeping both sides, so the page carried two nights' numbers under the
# same heading. markdownlint's MD024 was the only thing that noticed, in CI.
# Refusing the merge outright leaves the file whole and makes the resolution a
# generated file actually has -- take one side entire -- the easy one instead of the
# careful one. Measured on that same merge: 22 conflict markers become 0.
docs/guides/nightly_run.md -merge
docs/guides/benchmark_latest.md -merge
docs/guides/branch/*.md -merge
